`config` (#768, CC) is `config.v1`'s client side
(`spec/profiles/config/v1.md`), back on zk2 after FJ9 left v1's six verbs
dark: `get` reads a service's view or compact status with §5's answers,
`set` changes one group checked against the served schema first, and
`confirm`, `cancel`, `extend` and `persist` drive a pending change to its
end. A read exits 0 a document read, 1 one that does not decode, 2 nothing
read; an act 0 answered, 1 refused, 2 silence or an input refused here.

```console
$ zenctl config --help
A running service's configuration, through config.v1.

config.v1 (spec/profiles/config/v1.md), through a session in the deployment's namespace: `get` reads a service's view (the served schema beside every running value, where each came from, and what a restart would run) or its compact status, with what a tool may conclude from them (§5); `set` changes one group, its values typed and checked against the served schema before anything is sent; `confirm`, `cancel`, `extend` and `persist` drive a pending change to its end. Each act is its own key, so a deployment grants each separately (§2.21).

Usage: zenctl config <COMMAND>

Commands:
  get      Read a service's configuration: the view, or the compact status
  set      Change one group's parameters, checked against the served schema first
  confirm  Keep the pending change: it becomes the last change
  cancel   Undo the pending change now, as its deadline would
  extend   Move the pending change's deadline to now plus --confirm-s
  persist  Make the last change survive a restart: its own key, its own grant
  help     Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl config get --help
Read a service's configuration: the view, or the compact status.

One S4 GET of the owner's config.v1 view and status (core §4.2), or of the status alone (--status: two radio frames where the view takes six, §2.19), after presence and the descriptor (the premise of every answer: a service implements config.v1 when its descriptor lists it, §2.1). Each document is judged fresh, stale or unobservable by freshness.v1 at its 60 s horizon: a reply's stamp is aged only against a clock trusted to the HLC delta, measured on a live put during --for, or on the deployment's word, --clocks-synced. Then what a tool may conclude (§5), each answer yes, no, not yet, unobservable or not asked: is a change pending; did a change land and was its undo clean (--token); is each group writable; will each value survive a restart; has the owner converged to its desired document. Only a fresh document is the owner's word: a stale one, or a GET that drew nothing, answers nothing, never no. A sensitive value is never shown. Exit 0 a document read, 1 the owner answered with one that does not decode, 2 nothing read (silence, an absent service, one whose descriptor does not list config.v1).

Usage: zenctl config get [OPTIONS] <SYSTEM/SERVICE>

Arguments:
  <SYSTEM/SERVICE>
          The service, `<system>/<service>`

Options:
      --status
          Read the compact status alone: its values, instance, revision, floor, pending change, last change and desired report, never the view's schema (§2.19)

      --token <TOKEN>
          A change you made, by the token its set was answered with: did it land, and was its undo clean (§5)? Its events are read where a window hears them, or a union storage replays them

      --instance <ID>
          The instance that answered that set (the view's `instance`): a restart since is then a rollback this reader can name (§2.12)

      --desired-revision <REVISION>
          The revision of the desired document you put: has the owner converged to it (§2.18)? Default: the last document it acted on

      --for <SECS>
          Listen to the view, the status and the change events this many seconds before the GET: a put heard confirms its document on this host's clock and measures the owner's clock (freshness.v1 §2.5, §2.6). 31 hears every owner's re-put at least once

      --clocks-synced
          This host's clock and the owner's agree within the HLC delta (500 ms), as the deployment keeps them: a reply's stamp is then aged against this clock (freshness.v1 §2.6)

      --across-face
          The service sits across a constrained face (spec §8.5), where presence and descriptors do not cross: the deployment's word that it implements config.v1 (§2.19, R7)

      --namespace <NS>
          The deployment namespace: the session is opened in it.
          
          zk2 keys are base-relative (`zk2/<system>/<service>/…`), and a deployment's services run in a zenoh session namespace that prefixes them on the wire. A resolved verb opens its session in the same one, so it reads what a consumer of the deployment reads. `--base` is the same flag, and so is the active context's `base`. Resolution: flag > env > active context > empty — the bus-root deployment, no namespace. `zenctl namespace list` finds the namespaces in use.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_BASE=]
          [alias: --base]

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context from the config file for this invocation (default: the file's `current` pointer; env `ZENCTL_CONTEXT`)

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable (e.g. `tcp/127.0.0.1:7447`).
          
          The session is a zenoh client of these endpoints: no listener of its own, no gossip, nothing the mesh can route through. An endpoint nothing answers fails the command (exit 2) rather than reading as an empty bus, and one that does not parse is refused by name (exit 2).

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable — which makes the session a peer

      --scouting
          Enable multicast scouting (off by default, and think first)

      --timeout <SECS>
          Seconds to wait for replies (default 5; a context may override the default). Bounds the presence read, each descriptor GET and each contract retrieval, a state GET, and a call — which, given none, waits what its operation recommends (`timeout_ms`)

      --zenoh-config <FILE>
          Zenoh JSON5 config file: the passthrough that reaches a secured bus.
          
          Loaded as the base layer; --connect/--listen/--scouting apply on top when given. A file that sets a session namespace is refused: the namespace is `--namespace`'s to set.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_ZENOH_CONFIG=]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl config set --help
Change one group's parameters, checked against the served schema first.

The view is read first (S4), each NAME=VALUE typed as the kind the schema declares, and the change run through the owner's own checks (§2.5): what the owner would refuse is refused here, in its words, and nothing is sent (exit 2; --no-precheck sends it anyway, typed by spelling). A sensitive parameter is given only from a file or standard input, NAME=@FILE or NAME=@-, never inline, and is never echoed (§2.15); a text beginning with @ is written @@. A reach group can cut the link its reply travels on (§2.4): it needs --confirm-s (or --join), and the operator's yes at a terminal or --i-know from a script, as does any change with a window to a group whose class could not be read. A hot group answers `applied`, the read-back after the apply; a reach group `pending`, its token, apply_at and deadline on the owner's clock, before the apply: read the view over the new link, then `config confirm`. Exit 0 an answer of the reply type, 1 a refusal, 2 silence (a set that drew nothing started nothing a tool can name) or an input refused here.

Usage: zenctl config set [OPTIONS] <SYSTEM/SERVICE> <GROUP> [NAME=VALUE]...

Arguments:
  <SYSTEM/SERVICE>
          The service, `<system>/<service>`

  <GROUP>
          The group: the parameters that change together, the unit that has a class and that the key names (`@op/set/<GROUP>`)

  [NAME=VALUE]...
          The values, NAME=VALUE, NAME=@FILE or NAME=@- (standard input, once): a subset of the group changes those alone, and none at all is a change of nothing (§2.5)

Options:
      --confirm-s <SECS>
          The window, seconds: the change is undone at its deadline unless confirmed, the window raised to the owner's floor (§2.8). Required for a reach group, unless --join

      --join <TOKEN>
          Join the pending change by its token (§2.7): the group is applied under its window, confirmed, cancelled or undone with it. A joining set takes no window of its own, and does not move the deadline

      --expected-revision <REVISION>
          Refused unless the owner's revision is this one: the revision you read (§2.5, row 7)

      --idempotency-key <KEY>
          A key that makes a resend safe for 300 s: the owner answers its first answer again, and applies nothing twice (§2.14)

      --dry-run
          Check, and answer the view as it would read with these values applied, touching nothing (§2.9)

      --no-precheck
          Send without reading the view first: values typed by their spelling, nothing checked here, and the class unread, so a window asks for the operator's yes as a reach change does (§2.4)

      --across-face
          The service sits across a constrained face: the status is read first rather than the view (two frames, not six; values typed by its values, the class unread), and a silence is unattributed, since presence does not cross (§2.19, R7)

      --i-know
          Send a change that can cut the link without being asked: a reach group, or a window to a group whose class could not be read (§2.4)

      --actor <NAME>
          Who is asking, as the change event records it: claimed, never authentication; the access control is the authority (§2.17). Default: $USER, when set

      --request-id <ID>
          The request id the change event records. Default: a fresh ULID

      --namespace <NS>
          The deployment namespace: the session is opened in it.
          
          zk2 keys are base-relative (`zk2/<system>/<service>/…`), and a deployment's services run in a zenoh session namespace that prefixes them on the wire. A resolved verb opens its session in the same one, so it reads what a consumer of the deployment reads. `--base` is the same flag, and so is the active context's `base`. Resolution: flag > env > active context > empty — the bus-root deployment, no namespace. `zenctl namespace list` finds the namespaces in use.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_BASE=]
          [alias: --base]

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context from the config file for this invocation (default: the file's `current` pointer; env `ZENCTL_CONTEXT`)

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable (e.g. `tcp/127.0.0.1:7447`).
          
          The session is a zenoh client of these endpoints: no listener of its own, no gossip, nothing the mesh can route through. An endpoint nothing answers fails the command (exit 2) rather than reading as an empty bus, and one that does not parse is refused by name (exit 2).

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable — which makes the session a peer

      --scouting
          Enable multicast scouting (off by default, and think first)

      --timeout <SECS>
          Seconds to wait for replies (default 5; a context may override the default). Bounds the presence read, each descriptor GET and each contract retrieval, a state GET, and a call — which, given none, waits what its operation recommends (`timeout_ms`)

      --zenoh-config <FILE>
          Zenoh JSON5 config file: the passthrough that reaches a secured bus.
          
          Loaded as the base layer; --connect/--listen/--scouting apply on top when given. A file that sets a session namespace is refused: the namespace is `--namespace`'s to set.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_ZENOH_CONFIG=]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl config confirm --help
Keep the pending change: it becomes the last change.

config.v1 §2.10. Answered with the view after the act, or the compact status with --status. A confirm before every group of the change is applied is `busy`; one after the deadline, `busy` until the undo has run and `not_found` after. No control operation is idempotent: a confirm retried after a lost reply finds nothing pending, so read the last change instead (`config get --token`). Exit 0 answered, 1 refused, 2 silence.

Usage: zenctl config confirm [OPTIONS] <SYSTEM/SERVICE> <TOKEN>

Arguments:
  <SYSTEM/SERVICE>
          The service, `<system>/<service>`

  <TOKEN>
          The change's token, as its set was answered (or the view's `pending` and `last_change` show it)

Options:
      --status
          Answer with the compact status, not the view (`answer = "status"`): on a radio link the view is six frames, the status two (§2.10)

      --across-face
          The service sits across a constrained face: a silence is unattributed, since presence does not cross (§2.19, R7)

      --actor <NAME>
          Who is asking, as the change event records it: claimed, never authentication; the access control is the authority (§2.17). Default: $USER, when set

      --request-id <ID>
          The request id the change event records. Default: a fresh ULID

      --namespace <NS>
          The deployment namespace: the session is opened in it.
          
          zk2 keys are base-relative (`zk2/<system>/<service>/…`), and a deployment's services run in a zenoh session namespace that prefixes them on the wire. A resolved verb opens its session in the same one, so it reads what a consumer of the deployment reads. `--base` is the same flag, and so is the active context's `base`. Resolution: flag > env > active context > empty — the bus-root deployment, no namespace. `zenctl namespace list` finds the namespaces in use.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_BASE=]
          [alias: --base]

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context from the config file for this invocation (default: the file's `current` pointer; env `ZENCTL_CONTEXT`)

